        "text": "We're teaming up to accelerate open-source Al.\nOpenRouter is now integrated into Hugging Face directly. Same token, same endpoint, plus live model stats on the Hub and an inference widget we redesigned together.\nThe best of open, in one place. https://t.co/fNbvtJGSJB"
      },
      {
        "author_id": "2099789275495530496",
        "conversation_id": "2122465503813726427",
        "created_at": "2026-11-16T23:03:04.000Z",
        "edit_history_tweet_ids": [
          "2122464552273019032",
          "2122465503813726427"
        ],
        "id": "2122465503813726427",
        "lang": "en",
        "note_tweet": {
          "text": "Q:If Hugging Face is subpoenaed to take down a model, how does this play out for Pirateface, a GoDaddy/Hostinger domain on US servers from a Delaware holding company website where the USA seeders of this subpoenaed model are listed.\n\nA:Here is how that scenario typically plays out under US law and infrastructure realities.\n\n1. Hugging Face takedown  \n\nHugging Face is a US company. A valid subpoena, court order, or other legal process can compel them to remove a model from their platform. That immediately kills the official Hugging Face web seed that Pirate Face (and similar systems) rely on for initial downloads. The model page disappears or becomes unavailable from HF.\n\n2. The Pirate Face website itself  \n\npirateface. co is registered through GoDaddy (US registrar) and hosted on Amazon infrastructure in the United States (AS16509). Domain WHOIS is privacy-protected, which is common, but that does not shield the underlying registrant from legal process.\n\n- A US court order or subpoena can reach GoDaddy (as registrar) and the hosting provider.\n\n- Both can be compelled to disable the domain, take the site offline, or hand over account records, payment information, and logs.\n\n- A Delaware holding company or LLC provides some privacy and liability separation for day-to-day business, but it does not create immunity. Delaware entities are still subject to US federal process. Courts routinely pierce or compel disclosure through registered agents, payment trails, or related accounts. The corporate form does not block a properly issued subpoena.\n\nIn short: the website and domain are soft, US-reachable targets. They can be taken down or forced to delist specific models relatively quickly once legal process is served on the registrar, host, or entity.\n\n3. The US seeders listed on the site  \n\nThis is the more durable exposure.\n\n- BitTorrent trackers and DHT publish peer IP addresses. Anyone (including rights holders or law enforcement) can see which IPs are seeding a given infohash.\n\n- If the Pirate Face site or its tracker lists or facilitates those seeders, the site operator can be compelled to produce logs.\n\n- Individual US seeders can be identified via their ISP with a subpoena (standard practice in copyright and other cases involving BitTorrent). Their physical location inside the US makes them fully reachable by US process, independent of what happens to the website.\n\nThe swarm can continue with non-US peers after the site and HF web seed are gone, but any US seeder who was publicly listed or actively seeding remains identifiable and vulnerable on their own.\n\nBottom line  \n\n- HF removal kills the convenient web seed.\n\n- The GoDaddy + US-hosted website tied to a Delaware entity can be compelled to go offline or stop listing the model.\n\n- US seeders listed or participating are the hardest to protect; their IPs and ISP records are subject to ordinary US legal process.\n\nDelaware incorporation and domain privacy make casual discovery harder. They do not make the infrastructure or the US participants unreachable once a court is involved."
        },
